{
 "id": "u07",
 "n": 7,
 "level": "A1",
 "title": "gustar and similar verbs",
 "summary": "\"I like\" works the other way round in Spanish.",
 "explain": [
  {
   "h": "Backwards from English"
  },
  {
   "p": "*Me gusta el café* literally means \"coffee pleases me\". The thing you like is the subject, so the verb agrees with **the thing**, not with you."
  },
  {
   "table": [
    [
     "me gusta(n)",
     "I like"
    ],
    [
     "te gusta(n)",
     "you like"
    ],
    [
     "le gusta(n)",
     "he / she likes, you (usted) like"
    ],
    [
     "nos gusta(n)",
     "we like"
    ],
    [
     "os gusta(n)",
     "you all like"
    ],
    [
     "les gusta(n)",
     "they / you all (ustedes) like"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "p": "**gusta** + one thing or a verb · **gustan** + several things."
  },
  {
   "ex": [
    "Me gusta el trabajo.",
    "I like the job."
   ]
  },
  {
   "ex": [
    "Me gustan las ventanas grandes.",
    "I like big windows."
   ]
  },
  {
   "ex": [
    "Nos gusta viajar.",
    "We like travelling."
   ]
  },
  {
   "p": "To name the person, add **a + person**: *A mi novia le gusta el jazz.* For emphasis: *A mí me gusta, ¿y a ti?*"
  },
  {
   "h": "Same pattern"
  },
  {
   "p": "**encantar** (to love), **interesar** (to interest), **doler** (to hurt: *me duele la cabeza, me duelen los pies*), **parecer** (to seem: *me parece caro*), **importar** (to matter)."
  },
  {
   "tip": "Don't add *yo*: say *Me gusta*, never *Yo gusto*."
  }
 ],
